# 2. User Authentication Design

We decided to keep application users in MongoDB.

Example conceptual user document:

```json
{
  "username": "dharmendra",
  "password": "<BCrypt encoded password>",
  "role": "admin"
}
```

Possible roles:

```text
ADMIN
NORMAL
```

The password is **not stored as plain text**.

Instead, BCrypt is used:

```text
Raw password
     |
     v
BCryptPasswordEncoder
     |
     v
Encoded password
     |
     v
MongoDB
```

During login:

```text
Entered password
      |
      v
AuthenticationManager
      |
      v
BCrypt comparison
      |
      v
Authentication successful/failed
```

---

# 3. UserDetailsService

Spring Security needs a way to find a user.

We implemented a MongoDB-backed `UserDetailsService`.

Conceptually:

```java
@Service
public class MongoUserDetailsService implements UserDetailsService {

    @Override
    public UserDetails loadUserByUsername(String username) {

        // Find user from MongoDB

        // Convert Mongo user into Spring Security UserDetails

        return ...;
    }
}
```

Its responsibility is:

```text
username
   |
   v
MongoDB
   |
   v
Application User
   |
   v
Spring Security UserDetails
```

Important:

`UserDetailsService` does **not generate JWT**.

Its job is primarily to retrieve user information needed by Spring Security.

