# 9. The 403 Forbidden Problem

This was the most important debugging exercise.

We had successfully obtained a JWT.

Then we called:

```http
GET /employees/DKS002
Authorization: Bearer <JWT>
```

But the response was:

```text
403 Forbidden
```

Initially this was confusing because:

```text
Login worked
JWT was generated
JWT was sent
But protected endpoint failed
```

---

# 10. Important Debugging Observation

We checked the Spring Security logs.

During login we saw:

```text
Secured POST /auth/login
```

and:

```text
DaoAuthenticationProvider : Authenticated user
```

So login was working.

But for:

```text
GET /employees/DKS002
```

we saw:

```text
Securing GET /employees/DKS002
```

followed by:

```text
AnonymousAuthenticationFilter :
Set SecurityContextHolder to anonymous
```

and eventually:

```text
Http403ForbiddenEntryPoint :
Pre-authenticated entry point called. Rejecting access
```

This was the key clue.

Spring Security was treating the request as:

```text
anonymous
```

instead of:

```text
authenticated user
```

---

# 11. Root Cause of the 403

The JWT filter existed, but it was **not registered in the Spring Security filter chain**.

This is a very important Spring Security lesson:

> Creating a `JwtAuthenticationFilter` class is not enough.

Spring Security must actually execute that filter for incoming requests.

We therefore needed to register it:

```java
.addFilterBefore(
    jwtAuthenticationFilter,
    UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class
)
```

---

# 12. JwtAuthenticationFilter

We created a filter extending:

```java
OncePerRequestFilter
```

Conceptually:

```java
@Component
public class JwtAuthenticationFilter
        extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    private final JwtService jwtService;
    private final UserDetailsService userDetailsService;

    // constructor

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(
            HttpServletRequest request,
            HttpServletResponse response,
            FilterChain filterChain)
            throws ServletException, IOException {

        // Read Authorization header

        // Extract Bearer token

        // Validate JWT

        // Extract username

        // Load UserDetails

        // Create Authentication

        // Put Authentication into SecurityContext

        filterChain.doFilter(request, response);
    }
}
```

---

# 13. Why `@Component` Was Important

We initially encountered an error because:

```text
jwtAuthenticationFilter
```

was not available to `SecurityConfig`.

The solution was to make the filter a Spring bean:

```java
@Component
public class JwtAuthenticationFilter
        extends OncePerRequestFilter {
}
```

Now Spring creates the filter and can inject it into `SecurityConfig`.

For example:

```java
private final JwtAuthenticationFilter jwtAuthenticationFilter;

public SecurityConfig(
        JwtAuthenticationFilter jwtAuthenticationFilter) {

    this.jwtAuthenticationFilter =
            jwtAuthenticationFilter;
}
```

This teaches an important Spring concept:

```text
@Component
   |
   v
Spring IoC Container
   |
   v
Creates JwtAuthenticationFilter bean
   |
   v
Injects into SecurityConfig
```

---

# 14. Registering the JWT Filter

This fixed the main problem.

We added:

```java
.addFilterBefore(
    jwtAuthenticationFilter,
    UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class
)
```

The resulting conceptual security chain became:

```text
HTTP Request
     |
     v
JwtAuthenticationFilter
     |
     v
UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter
     |
     v
Authorization filters
     |
     v
Controller
```

The exact complete Spring Security chain contains many filters, but our important application-specific filter is the JWT filter.

---

# 15. What JwtAuthenticationFilter Actually Does

The filter has several responsibilities.

### Step 1 — Read header

It checks:

```http
Authorization: Bearer eyJ...
```

### Step 2 — Extract token

From:

```text
Bearer eyJ...
```

it extracts:

```text
eyJ...
```

### Step 3 — Extract username

The JWT contains:

```json
{
  "sub": "dharmendra"
}
```

So the filter gets:

```text
dharmendra
```

### Step 4 — Load user

The filter calls:

```java
userDetailsService.loadUserByUsername(username)
```

which eventually gets the user from MongoDB.

### Step 5 — Validate JWT

`JwtService` checks that the token is valid and belongs to the expected user.

### Step 6 — Create Authentication

Spring Security needs an `Authentication` object.

Conceptually:

```text
JWT
 |
 v
username
 |
 v
UserDetails
 |
 v
Authentication
```

### Step 7 — Put it into SecurityContext

This is critical:

```java
SecurityContextHolder
```

is populated with the authenticated user.

Conceptually:

```text
SecurityContextHolder
       |
       v
Authentication
       |
       +---- username
       |
       +---- authorities
```

### Step 8 — Continue request

Finally:

```java
filterChain.doFilter(request, response);
```

passes the request to the remaining filters/controller.

